1. Intuition

We need to find the longest prefix that is common to all strings.

For example:

["flower", "flow", "flight"]

The common prefix is:

"fl"

The key observation is:

After sorting all the strings alphabetically, we only need to compare the first and last strings.

Why?

Suppose we sort:

["flower", "flow", "flight"]

After sorting:

["flight", "flow", "flower"]

The first string is "flight" and the last is "flower".

If these two strings have a common prefix "fl", then every string between them must also have that prefix.

So instead of comparing every string, we compare only:

first = v[0]
last = v[n-1]
2. Approach
Step 1: Create an answer string
string ans = "";

This will store the common prefix.

Step 2: Sort the strings
sort(v.begin(), v.end());

For example:

Before:
["flower", "flow", "flight"]

After:
["flight", "flow", "flower"]
Step 3: Get the first and last strings
int n = v.size();

string first = v[0];
string last = v[n-1];

So:

first = "flight"
last  = "flower"

These are the two strings we need to compare.

Step 4: Compare characters

We can only compare up to the length of the shorter string:

for(int i = 0; i < min(first.size(), last.size()); i++)

For:

first = "flight"
last  = "flower"

Compare:

f == f ✓
l == l ✓
i != o ✗

So the answer is:

"fl"
Step 5: If characters are different
if(first[i] != last[i]) {
    return ans;
}

As soon as we find a mismatch, there cannot be any longer common prefix.

Step 6: If characters are equal
ans += first[i];

We add the matching character to ans.

For example:

f → ans = "f"
l → ans = "fl"
Step 7: Return the answer

If the loop finishes without finding a mismatch:

return ans;

This handles cases where one string is completely a prefix of another.

Example:

["flower", "flow", "flow"]

The comparison stops at the length of "flow" and returns:

"flow"



class Solution {
public:
    string longestCommonPrefix(vector<string>& v) {
        string ans="";
        sort(v.begin(),v.end());
        int n=v.size();
        string first=v[0],last=v[n-1];
        for(int i=0;i<min(first.size(),last.size());i++){
            if(first[i]!=last[i]){
                return ans;
            }
            ans+=first[i];
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n log n + m)

Space Complexity:

O(1)
